## Before class

1. **cbeams — [*How to Write a Git Commit Message*](https://cbea.ms/git-commit/)** — **the primary reading.** The seven rules, and the argument behind them: a project's history is documentation that only works if someone cared about it at the time. Learn the seven rules; we'll grade eight real commit messages against them in class.
2. **Google — [*The Standard of Code Review*](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/standard.html)** and **[*What to Look For in a Code Review*](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/looking-for.html)** — two short pages from Google's engineering-practices guide, written for reviewers. Then skim the **[author's side](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/developer/)**, especially *small changes* and *writing a good description*. You'll write your first review comments in class.
3. **Nothing to bring today but the reading.** Wednesday is a hands-on lab, and there's a list at the bottom of this page for that.

